## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to implement per-tenant routing through ConfigMaps, path rewriting, and versioning with safe defaults.

## Key Concepts
- Namespace-scoped ConfigMaps for feature flags
- Request rewriting via tenant header
- Versioning strategy and default fallback
- Minimal YAML manifests for end-to-end validation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo rewrite function
function rewrite(req, tenantId, version, mapping) {
  const base = mapping[tenantId] || mapping['default'];
  req.url = `/v${version || '1'}/api${req.path}`;
  req.headers['Accept'] = 'application/json';
  req.headers['Host'] = base.host;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test unknown tenants and version upgrades?
- How to handle partial mappings and rollback?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical security design in a service-mMesh context, focusing on per-tenant egress control, runtime policy evaluation, and rollout strategy—uncovering depth beyond static docs.

## Key Concepts
- SPIFFE IDs and workload labeling for tenant isolation
- Istio EgressGateway + AuthorizationPolicy for egress enforcement
- OPA-backed policy store for dynamic per-tenant allowlists
- Observable tenant badge surface in gateway status and rollout signals

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example JS-like policy evaluation (illustrative only)
const canAccess = (tenant, endpoint) => {
  const allowlist = {
    'tenant-a': ['telemetry.example.com','updates.example.com'],
    'tenant-b': ['telemetry.example.org']
  }
  return (allowlist[tenant] || []).includes(endpoint)
}
```
